  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nUpper troughing over the eastern CONUS, and embedded shortwaves \nmoving through the Ohio valley, will help to push a cold front \nthrough our area over the next 24 hours or so. It's currently \nsituated well to our northwest, but it will be approaching from the \nnorthwest after daybreak tomorrow, finally pushing in the southern \nparts of the CWA or perhaps completely south of the area by late \nWednesday night. The main concern with this front will be the \npotential for training storms that develop during the pre-dawn hours \nlater tonight and the associated flooding risks that occur, and then \nwhether or not additional convection fires along the front before it \nmoves out of our area Wednesday night for a flood risk in our \nsouthern CWA.\n\nFor the pre-dawn hours overnight, several runs of CAM guidance this \nafternoon and evening have been pointing towards development of \nshowers and thunderstorms across the northern TN valley and into our \nVA counties as forcing ahead of the cold front coincide with \nconfluent and front-parallel H85 flow move into that region. This \nwould support training convection along/ahead of the front, yielding \nthe potential for high rainfall amounts. HREF probabilities show 30 \nto 40 percent odds for 6hr rainfall amounts to exceed 2\" while \nseveral HRRR runs this evening have shown explicit deterministic \nrainfall amounts in the 3-5\" range somewhere over the northern TN \nvalley and northern East Tennessee mountains. All of this yields \nconfidence that there is still a risk of flash flooding tonight. \nBelieve the flash flood watch in place adequately covers the risk \narea. \n\nThere is some concern about whether an additional watch, or perhaps \nan expansion of the current watch, will be necessary for Wednesday \nnight. As the front slowly shifts south through the region tomorrow, \nredevelopment of convection along the front is likely. Again, storm \nmotions will be parallel to the front, giving continued risk of \ntraining storms and locally high rainfall amounts. At this time, \nthere remains enough uncertainty about the location of the front \nwhen that next round of storms fires in earnest. It could be in our \nfar southern areas, or it could be just south of our forecast area. \nIt's even possible that the threat affects our southern tier \ncounties and lingers into Thursday if the front struggles to fully \npush south of our CWA. For these reasons, I'd prefer to hold off on \nexpanding the current watch or issuing a new one to avoid confusion \nand allow details to become a little more clear. \n\nThe remainder of the forecast has the basic pattern of upper ridging \nto our west and troughing over the eastern CONUS continuing.  We \nwill see short wave energy moving southeast on the back side of the \ntrough at times that will bring additional showers an storms to our \narea for the Friday through Tuesday period.  Timing of these \nfeatures and the resulting convection is of course unclear this far \nout. Temperatures for Thursday into Saturday look to be a bit below \nnormal, then the remainder of the period through Tuesday will likely \nsee temperatures not far from normal for this time of year.",
